WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.427
This query recreates the patient analytics view with security invoker enabled.

00:00:04.427 --> 00:00:08.027
It still exposes the same selected analytical columns.

00:00:08.027 --> 00:00:12.547
The key difference is how PostgreSQL evaluates underlying access permissions.

00:00:12.547 --> 00:00:16.417
In a production environment, you would test this with real roles and grants.

00:00:16.417 --> 00:00:20.687
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:26.122
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:26.122 --> 00:00:28.277
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:28.277 --> 00:00:33.817
In a real setting such as a data platform materializing expensive summaries for faster consumption,

00:00:33.817 --> 00:00:40.092
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:40.092 --> 00:00:40.497
before discussing syntax.

00:00:40.497 --> 00:00:45.920
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
